      "pronunciationText": "Last time, we saw that the rows and columns of the periodic table follow electron shells. Here's a puzzle from that table. Sodium and chlorine sit in the same row. A chlorine atom has six more protons and six more electrons than a sodium atom. So you'd expect it to be bigger. It's not. Chlorine's atom is much smaller. And go down a column, from lithium to potassium, and atoms get larger. So why does atomic radius change across a period and down a group?",
