      "pronunciationText": "Two things decide how close the outer electrons sit. The first is the nucleus. Its positive protons attract the negative electrons, and more protons pull harder. The second is shielding. Inner electrons sit between the nucleus and the outer electrons, and their repulsion cancels part of the nucleus's pull. A simple way to estimate the pull an outer electron feels is the core charge: the number of protons, minus the number of inner electrons. For sodium, that's eleven minus ten, so plus one. Exam answers call this net pull the effective nuclear charge.",
