      "peakFraction": 0.861083984375,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Atoms can also gain or lose electrons to form ions. When sodium loses its one outer electron, it doesn't just lose a dot. It loses the whole third shell. Its outer electrons are now in the second shell, and the sodium ion is much smaller. Chlorine goes the other way. It gains one electron into its outer shell, but the nucleus still has seventeen protons. The extra electron adds repulsion between the outer electrons, so they spread out, and the chloride ion is larger.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
