      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "So is it all about how many electrons there are? Look at five ions that each have exactly ten electrons: oxide, fluoride, sodium, magnesium and aluminium ions. They're called isoelectronic, which means same number of electrons. Their sizes still fall, because the number of protons rises, from eight up to thirteen. More protons pull the same ten electrons in tighter. So electron count alone never tells you the size. Protons and shells matter too.",
      "humanListening": "pending"
